a new era in American spaceflight  starting just before sunrise on  Florida's Space Coast a SpaceX rocket  equipped with the first commercially  developed crew capsule began a critical  test this morning when it blasted off  toward the International Space Station  there was no one on board but that  capsule could be used to take astronauts  into space later this year  mark Strassmann was there for the launch  he joins us now from the Kennedy Space  Center wow this is some exciting thing  to happen what it could happen what it  could mean for for all the rest of us  good morning I got to tell you right  around three o'clock this morning the  skies were on here just lit up along  with the moods of thousands of people  who had gathered here to watch this  launch this moment represents the  beginning of a new space age in which  companies rather than countries will  lead the way to what here liftoff the  Falcon 9 rocket and his crew Dragon  capsule soared into history this morning  bringing America a giant leap closer to  completely homegrown space travel for  the first time in nearly eight years ten  minutes after launch the Falcon 9 rocket  stage successfully touched down on a  recovery ship 300 miles off the Florida  coast by then the crew Dragon capsule  was already orbiting earth at five miles  per second SpaceX founder Elon Musk a  little emotionally exhausted because  super stressful but it worked so far  through next Friday the crew dragon  needs to prove it can safely dock and  undock autonomously with a space station  re-enter the Earth's atmosphere and  splashdown off the Florida coast if all  goes well the SpaceX capsule could carry  two astronauts as early as this summer I  want to be clear our goal here is safety  NASA Administrator Jim bridenstine told  us NASA is no longer in the space race  business it is less relevant as far as  the date what is more relevant is the  safety of our crew so we're gonna make  sure we do things right period end of  story on this mission the only passenger  is a dummy a smart dummy named Ripley  outfitted with sensors to measure the  impact of the ride for its eventual  first fliers NASA astronauts Bob Behnken  and Doug Hurley so far so good so from  our standpoint this is this is what you  want to see you want to see the team  hitting its stride seeing a success like  this definitely gives us a lot of  confidence in the future just before  daybreak tomorrow morning the crew  dragon will dock with the International  Space Station at NASA and SpaceX just  have to hope that docking is as  picture-perfect as this morning's launch  Dana mark thank you for those of us that  remember the space shuttle or the Apollo  launches and gathering around the TV it  is really exciting to see this again
 

